        </How>

        <Summary>
            <ul>
                <li>Reference the AppHost and start one isolated resource graph for distributed tests.</li>
                <li>Bound startup with cancellation, wait for health, and verify an actual business operation.</li>
                <li>Use generated endpoints, unique test data, and Playwright's auto-waiting locators.</li>
                <li>Prove API-to-database-to-UI behavior without relying on fixed ports or production data.</li>
                <li>For Kafka or multiple repositories, test observable contracts rather than resource startup alone.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>What to read next:</strong> revisit
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet/">getting started with Aspire in .NET</a>
                to adjust the AppHost resource graph before expanding your test suite.
            </p>
        </Summary>
